/**
 * \mainpage mplapack-interop developer reference
 *
 * \section architecture Architecture
 *
 * The public GNU Octave layer is in inst/ and the mp method directory.  It implements the
 * normal Octave syntax and owns compatibility boundaries.  The native bridge
 * is in src/octave_bridge.cc and dispatches to storage, precision, arithmetic,
 * MPLAPACK, serialization, RNG, interpolation, solver, quadrature,
 * optimization, and graphics helpers.
 *
 * \section storage Storage and value semantics
 *
 * An mp value contains one native RAII payload: MPFR for real data or MPC for
 * complex data, together with shape and stored precision.  Public values are
 * immutable.  Dense matrices are contiguous column-major storage and are not
 * arrays of scalar wrapper objects.  Native code must copy a public value
 * before a destructive LAPACK call.
 *
 * \section precision Precision policy
 *
 * There is one project-owned process-local default used for newly constructed
 * values.  Existing values retain their stored precision when mpbits changes.
 * An operation selects one p_op from its operands, enters the corresponding
 * MPFR/MPC execution scope, normalizes temporaries to p_op, and restores the
 * caller's backend context on all exits.  A new worker thread does not inherit
 * a parent thread's precision automatically; worker entry must establish its
 * own scope.
 *
 * \section backends MPLAPACK and MPFR/MPC
 *
 * Dense BLAS/LAPACK operations dispatch to the reference MPLAPACK MPFR/MPC
 * backend through pkg-config.  Real-only operations remain on real R* paths.
 * Complex operations promote once to MPC at p_op.  No supported numerical
 * path is allowed to use an implicit builtin binary64 fallback.  The only
 * ordinary double boundary is final data conversion for graphics.
 *
 * \section ownership Ownership and errors
 *
 * Helpers return operation-owned values or copies.  Octave callbacks are
 * checked at their boundary; nonlinear, quadrature, and optimization
 * callbacks must return mp values.  Serialization records a versioned text
 * schema rather than native addresses or MPFR limb bytes.
 *
 * \section tests Tests
 *
 * Focused tests live under test/ and are grouped by M, C, N, S, and T
 * milestones.  The documentation example runner executes the files under
 * examples/;
 * test/t00_schur_qz.tst contains the Grcar and QZ reconstruction contract.
 * See docs/backend-map.md and docs/public-api-inventory.md for the public
 * entry-to-backend map.
 *
 * \section neig Difficult nonsymmetric eigensystem example
 *
 * examples/13_nonsymmetric_eig_suite.m and the helpers under
 * examples/nonsymmetric_eig/ form a repository-local QA harness on top of
 * the existing public mp/eig API. It constructs exact or independently
 * referenced nonnormal matrices, runs balance and nobalance, and measures
 * the returned right and left relations at high MP precision. The harness
 * preserves stored precision and ambient-default restoration; its native
 * double rows are explicit rounded-input controls. Result serialization
 * retains MP text, while the optional PNG is a display-only boundary.
 *
 * \section neigt_tiers NEIGT verified tiers
 *
 * The numbered examples examples/14_neig_tier_s.m through
 * examples/17_neig_verified_va.m form the manifest-driven NEIGT Tier-S,
 * Tier-A, V-S, and V-A QA surface. They use the existing public mp/eig/svd/
 * qr/solve interfaces and example-local audited outward checkers; they do not
 * add an installed numerical API. Generation precision, frozen inputs,
 * candidates, and certificates are separate records. V proofs require their
 * explicit preconditions, count all roots, preserve nontrivial defective
 * clusters/block Schur claims, and never use residuals or precision agreement
 * as a substitute for an enclosure. Exact proof bundles are written by
 * mp_neig_write_outputs and replayed by mp_neig_replay without calling eig or
 * reconstructing an ideal matrix. All MP arithmetic follows the operation
 * precision and no implicit binary64 fallback is permitted.
 */
